    "body": "Ministro Hales plant. ( Image: Codelco | Flickr. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 19, 2026\nChile’s environmental regulator has accused state-owned copper producer Codelco of two serious dust-control violations at its Ministro Hales mine, exposing the operation to fines, closure or the loss of its environmental permit.\nThe Superintendencia del Medio Ambiente (SMA) announced the charges Thursday following inspections and seven public complaints submitted between 2021 and 2024 in Calama, a northern Chilean city designated as exceeding legal limits for inhalable particulate pollution. Each charge carries a maximum fine of 5,000 annual tax units, equivalent to about $4.3 million (C$6.2 million), for combined potential fines of $8.7 million.\n“Calama is an area declared saturated with respirable particulate matter, so strict compliance with emissions-control measures is essential to protect public health,” environmental superintendent Emanuel Ibarra said in a Spanish-language statement.\nThe enforcement action follows environmental approval in January for a $2.8-billion project to extend Ministro Hales’ operating life to 2054. Codelco said the expansion would lift annual copper output to about 200,000 tonnes from roughly 170,000 tonnes.\nDust control failures\nThe SMA alleges that Codelco operated the mine’s primary crusher without the baghouse filtration system required under its environmental approvals. The regulator also alleges that dust-removal efficiency fell below the mandated 75% between November 2023 and February 2024.\nA second charge concerns Codelco’s alleged failure to apply bischofite, a magnesium chloride-based dust suppressant, to inactive areas of its waste-rock dump during 2023 and 2024. The treatment was intended to reduce wind erosion by 85%.\nThat charge also cites missing records documenting implementation of the dust-control measures between 2023 and 2025.\nBoth alleged breaches were classified as serious under Chilean environmental law. The charges are not final findings of wrongdoing, and any sanctions will depend on the outcome of the proceedings.\nTailings compliance\nIn a separate decision, the SMA approved a compliance program submitted by Codelco to address groundwater infiltration and tailings-management violations at the Talabre tailings facility.\nThe regulator had filed charges in August 2024 after finding that the company failed to implement a contingency plan in response to changes in groundwater conditions and deposited tailings with a solids content below the required 60%.\nThe approved program includes eight measures to be implemented over 24 months at an estimated cost of 66.9 billion Chilean pesos ($66.8 million).\nAmong the main commitments, Codelco will install 22 additional extraction wells to strengthen the facility’s hydraulic containment barrier, which is monitored through 17 wells.\nThe company will also implement its PRECLA tailings preclassification project to achieve the required solids concentration and conduct water-capture tests within the tailings basin.\nApproval of the program suspends the earlier enforcement proceedings while Codelco carries out the agreed measures. If the company fails to meet its commitments, the SMA can reopen the case and impose fines of up to twice those otherwise applicable to the original violations. Print\nTAG: Chile mining copper production environment\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: South America\nCOMPANY: Codelco\nRepublish this article",
